        <li><a href="#types-of-gst-supplies" class="text-link hover:underline">3. Types of GST Supplies in Singapore</a></li>
        <li><a href="#calculate-9-percent-gst" class="text-link hover:underline">4. How to Calculate 9% GST (Formula & Examples)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#iras-compliance-payment" class="text-link hover:underline">5. IRAS GST Login, Filing & Paying Online</a></li>
        <li><a href="#global-vat-comparison" class="text-link hover:underline">6. Singapore GST vs Regional & Global VAT Rates</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faqs" class="text-link hover:underline">7. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <h2 id="what-is-singapore-gst" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. What is Singapore GST & How It Works</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>Goods and Services Tax (GST)</strong> in Singapore—known in Malay as <em>GST Singapura</em> or Bahasa Indonesia as <em>GST Singapore adalah</em> tax konsumsi—is a broad-based consumption tax levied on the import of goods, as well as nearly all supplies of goods and services in Singapore.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are searching <em>"singapore gst how many percent"</em> or <em>"singapore gst how much"</em>, the standard rate as enforced by the <strong>Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore (IRAS)</strong> is <strong>9%</strong>. In global terminology, Singapore GST is equivalent to Value-Added Tax (VAT), sales tax, or consumption tax in other jurisdictions.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Chart: GST Flow Diagram -->
    <div class="my-6 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink mb-4 text-center dark:text-on-primary">Visual Flow of Singapore GST Collection Framework</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-center text-sm">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-slate-700 dark:bg-slate-800">
          <div class="font-bold text-primary mb-1">Step 1: Import / Production</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted dark:text-muted">GST (9%) paid on raw materials or customs entry via Singapore Customs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-slate-700 dark:bg-slate-800">
          <div class="font-bold text-primary mb-1">Step 2: Business Supply</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted dark:text-muted">GST registered businesses charge 9% Output Tax and claim 9% Input Tax on business purchases.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-slate-700 dark:bg-slate-800">
          <div class="font-bold text-primary mb-1">Step 3: End Consumer</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-muted dark:text-muted">Final consumer pays 9% GST. Net tax revenue remitted to Singapore Government Treasury.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <h2 id="gst-rate-history-timeline" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. Singapore GST Rate Hike History (3% to 9%)</h2>
    <p>
      Many taxpayers ask about the <strong>last GST increase in Singapore</strong>, <em>"singapore gst 5 to 7"</em>, <em>"singapore gst increase to 8"</em>, and <em>"when was the 9 gst singapore implementation"</em>. Since its inception in April 1994, the Singapore government GST rate has undergone structured statutory revisions to fund healthcare infrastructure, social safety nets, and economic growth for an aging population.
    </p>

    <!-- Table: Historical Timeline -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft font-semibold text-ink dark:bg-surface dark:text-on-primary">
          <tr>
            <th class="border p-3">Implementation Period</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Statutory GST Rate</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Government Policy Context</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Key Budget Announcement</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline dark:divide-overlay-lighter">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">1 April 1994 – 31 December 2002</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">3%</td>
            <td class="p-3">Initial introduction of GST in Singapore to broaden the tax base.</td>
            <td class="p-3">Budget 1993</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">1 January 2003 – 31 December 2003</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">4%</td>
            <td class="p-3">Phased fiscal adjustment following Asian financial recovery.</td>
            <td class="p-3">Budget 2002</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">1 January 2004 – 30 June 2007</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">5%</td>
            <td class="p-3">Fiscal consolidation and corporate tax rate reduction.</td>
            <td class="p-3">Budget 2003</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">1 July 2007 – 31 December 2022</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">7%</td>
            <td class="p-3">Maintained at 7% for 15 years to support healthcare & social spending.</td>
            <td class="p-3">Budget 2007</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">1 January 2023 – 31 December 2023</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-amber-600">8%</td>
            <td class="p-3">First step of two-tier GST rate hike (8% GST Singapore).</td>
            <td class="p-3">Budget 2022</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-primary/5 font-semibold dark:bg-primary/10">
            <td class="p-3 text-primary">1 January 2024 – Present (2026)</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-extrabold text-primary text-base">9%</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Final tier of GST increase (9% GST Singapore rate maintained through 2026).</td>
            <td class="p-3">Budget 2022 / 2024 / 2026</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      For those wondering about <strong>GST 2026 Singapore rate changes</strong> or <em>"sg gst rate increase 2026"</em>: the Singapore Ministry of Finance has confirmed that the <strong>9% GST rate</strong> will remain stable through 2026, with no further rate hikes planned in the immediate fiscal pipeline.
    </p>

    <!-- Section 3 -->
    <h2 id="types-of-gst-supplies" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">3. Types of GST Supplies in Singapore</h2>
    <p>
      IRAS categorizes transactions into four primary tax treatments under Singapore tax legislation:
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
          <span class="rounded bg-primary/10 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-primary">Standard-Rated (9%)</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mb-2 dark:text-on-primary">Standard Domestic Supplies</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">
          Most goods sold and services rendered in Singapore attract the standard 9% GST. Examples include retail goods, dining, professional advisory, domestic transport, and imported goods.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
          <span class="rounded bg-emerald-500/10 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-emerald-600">Zero-Rated (0%)</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mb-2 dark:text-on-primary">Exports & International Services</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">
          Exported physical goods and international services (e.g. cross-border freight, services provided to overseas clients) are taxed at 0%. Businesses can still claim back input GST incurred.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
          <span class="rounded bg-slate-500/10 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Exempt Supplies (0%)</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mb-2 dark:text-on-primary">Financial & Property Transactions</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">
          Exempt supplies include provision of financial services (interest, loan issuance), sale and lease of residential properties, and investment precious metals (IPM gold, silver, platinum).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
          <span class="rounded bg-purple-500/10 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-bold text-purple-600">Out of Scope</span>
        </div>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink mb-2 dark:text-on-primary">Non-Taxable Transactions</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-body dark:text-muted">
          Third-country sales (goods transferred from country A to B without touching Singapore), private transactions, and statutory government regulatory fees.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 4 -->
    <h2 id="calculate-9-percent-gst" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">4. How to Calculate 9% GST (Formula & Examples)</h2>
    <p>
      Calculating Singapore GST depends on whether your baseline price is <strong>GST-exclusive</strong> (tax added on top) or <strong>GST-inclusive</strong> (tax embedded inside the total price).
    </p>

    <!-- Calculation Formulas Card -->
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-6 dark:border-overlay-light dark:bg-surface">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-4 dark:text-on-primary">Official Singapore GST Formulas (9% Rate)</h3>
      <div class="space-y-4 font-mono text-sm">
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-canvas p-4 border border-hairline dark:bg-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800">
          <div class="font-bold text-primary font-sans mb-1">1. GST Exclusive Formula (Add 9% to Net Price)</div>
          <code>GST Amount = Net Price × 0.09</code><br/>
          <code>Total Invoice Price = Net Price × 1.09</code>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-canvas p-4 border border-hairline dark:bg-slate-900 dark:border-slate-800">
          <div class="font-bold text-primary font-sans mb-1">2. GST Inclusive Formula (Extract 9% from Gross Price)</div>
          <code>GST Amount = Gross Price × (9 / 109) = Gross Price × 0.0825688</code><br/>
          <code>Net Base Price = Gross Price × (100 / 109)</code>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4">
      Try our interactive <a href="/countries/singapore/gst/gst-calculator/" class="text-link font-semibold hover:underline">Singapore GST Calculator</a> to calculate net amounts, tax components, and gross totals instantly!
    </p>

    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <h2 id="iras-compliance-payment" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">5. IRAS GST Login, Filing & Paying Online</h2>
    <p>
      All GST-registered companies in Singapore must file quarterly or monthly <strong>Form F5 Returns</strong> electronically via the IRAS <strong>myTax Portal</strong> (<a href="https://mytax.iras.gov.sg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline">mytax.iras.gov.sg</a>).
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How to Pay GST Online in Singapore</h3>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
      <li>
        <strong>Log in to myTax Portal:</strong> Authenticate using your company's Corppass credentials to access your GST portal account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>File Form F5 GST Return:</strong> Declare total taxable supplies, output tax collected, and allowable input tax claimed for the accounting period.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Submit Online Payment:</strong> Pay the net GST payable within one month from the end of the accounting period. Supported payment channels include:
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-1 space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li><strong>GIRO:</strong> Automated direct debit with an extended payment deadline (up to 15th of the following month).</li>
          <li><strong>PayNow QR:</strong> Instant electronic transfer via corporate banking apps.</li>
          <li><strong>Internet Banking / FAST / Telegraphic Transfer:</strong> Direct bill payment using IRAS Virtual Account number.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Section 6 -->
    <h2 id="global-vat-comparison" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">6. Singapore GST vs Regional & Global VAT Rates</h2>
    <p>
      Even after increasing to 9%, Singapore maintains one of the most competitive consumption tax rates among developed economies globally, making it a compelling hub for business regional headquarters and logistics operations.
    </p>

    <!-- Comparative Table -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft font-semibold text-ink dark:bg-surface dark:text-on-primary">
          <tr>
